(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-ocular-trauma-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Ocular Trauma Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient with ocular trauma is intoxicated and cannot give a history. Where should the account come from?","choices":["The examination findings alone","The transferring service's diagnosis","Family, friends or bystanders","Waiting until the patient sobers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The circumstance of injury still has to be established. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the limitation of computed tomography of the orbits in basilar skull fracture?","choices":["It cannot be done in an unconscious patient","The fracture is not always evident on it","It is contraindicated after head trauma","It requires contrast that may be nephrotoxic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A normal scan does not exclude it. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which feature of orbital floor fracture is specific to children?","choices":["Children always have marked periorbital swelling","Children need no imaging","Entrapment with no soft tissue signs","Entrapment never occurs before adolescence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the white-eyed blowout. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What position is the patient placed in while awaiting repair of a retinal detachment?","choices":["Head of bed elevated 30 to 40 degrees","Flat, with the head lower than the body","Prone with the face down","Sitting fully upright"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside pain control and antiemetics. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which action must be avoided whenever ocular trauma is suspected?","choices":["Dilating the eye","Asking about tetanus status","Inspecting the periorbital tissues","Testing confrontation fields"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It removes the pupil examination, one of the few windows onto the inside of the eye. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man arrives after a workshop explosion with an obviously injured right eye and noisy breathing. What comes first?","choices":["Visual acuity in both eyes","A shield over the injured eye","Ophthalmology consultation","Airway, breathing and circulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The eye waits until the patient is stable. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which history makes globe rupture more likely at lower force?","choices":["Long-standing myopia alone","Seasonal allergic conjunctivitis","Previous corneal abrasion","Previous intraocular surgery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Old incisions leave a permanent weak point. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which drugs should be avoided in a patient with hyphema?","choices":["Aspirin and antiplatelets","Antiemetics","Cycloplegic drops","Acetazolamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They raise the risk of a secondary haemorrhage. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which condition raises the risk of complications from hyphema?","choices":["Seasonal allergic rhinitis","Hypothyroidism","Iron deficiency anaemia","Sickle cell disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is specifically flagged as raising the risk. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"When is intraocular pressure NOT measured in a patient with hyphema?","choices":["When the hyphema fills less than half the chamber","When the injury was more than a day ago","Pressure is measured in every case","When a penetrating globe injury is suspected"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pressing on an open globe risks extruding its contents. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the aim of hyphema management?","choices":["Preventing infection","Reducing photophobia","Preventing a rebleed","Clearing the blood as fast as possible"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Every element of the regimen serves that. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why are antiemetics given alongside analgesia in a suspected open globe?","choices":["They shorten the time to surgical repair","Vomiting raises intraocular pressure","They reduce the risk of endophthalmitis","They lower the intraocular pressure directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Keeping the patient from straining protects the eye. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"How is a periorbital haematoma decompressed?","choices":["Observation until it resorbs","Canthotomy with cantholysis","Pressure patching for 24 hours","Intravenous mannitol alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Release the lateral canthal tendon and cut its inferior branch so blood can drain. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"In a true blowout fracture, which wall gives way most often?","choices":["The posterior wall","The superior orbital rim","The orbital floor","The lateral wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Raised orbital pressure blows out the floor most frequently, or the medial wall. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A laceration has cut the lens capsule. What happens to the lens?","choices":["It dislocates posteriorly in every case","It becomes hydrated, oedematous and opaque","It hardens and darkens","It is unaffected if the cut is peripheral"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aqueous entering the capsule clouds it. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A patient has an orbital contusion after a blow to the face. What must be excluded?","choices":["Retinoblastoma","Brain trauma","Thyroid eye disease","Chronic open-angle glaucoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The force that bruised the orbit reaches the brain. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Why is surgery for an orbital fracture often delayed one to two weeks?","choices":["To complete a course of antibiotics","To wait for the diplopia to resolve spontaneously","To let orbital swelling settle first","To allow the fracture to begin healing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Operating into a swollen orbit raises the pressure further. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 30-year-old has a distorted pupil pulled toward a corneal wound, a flat anterior chamber and a soft eye. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Orbital contusion","Open globe injury","Corneal abrasion","Subconjunctival haemorrhage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Those three findings together define a full-thickness defect. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which bedside test may be positive when cerebrospinal fluid is present?","choices":["Fluorescein staining","A rapid streptococcal antigen test","A dextrose stick","A urine dipstick for protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cerebrospinal fluid contains glucose. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which event usually precedes a rhegmatogenous retinal detachment?","choices":["Corneal ulceration","Posterior vitreous detachment","Central retinal artery occlusion","Acute angle closure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The vitreous separates first, then traction opens a retinal break. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which feature makes computed tomography without contrast automatic after trauma?","choices":["Loss of consciousness","Subconjunctival haemorrhage alone","Photophobia","A foreign body sensation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It sits alongside alcohol, confusion, tachypnoea, apnoeic breathing, anticoagulants and eye penetration. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Muscle entrapment is confirmed on computed tomography. Who is called, and why?","choices":["A facial trauma surgeon urgently","Ophthalmology within 2 to 3 days","Neurosurgery for admission","No consult; ice and analgesia suffice"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A compromised blood supply is the reason for the urgency. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A patient who was grinding metal has vertical linear scratches across the cornea. What does that indicate?","choices":["Order an orbital radiograph","Refer immediately for surgical exploration","Evert the upper lid to find the object","Examine the lower fornix first"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vertical linear defects mean it is in the tarsal conjunctiva of the upper lid. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which type of retinal detachment is the most common?","choices":["Traction","Exudative","Congenital","Rhegmatogenous"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Full-thickness retinal breaks let liquefied vitreous behind the retina. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A patient with a basilar skull fracture has a cerebrospinal fluid leak. What follows?","choices":["Neurosurgery consult and admission","Discharge with outpatient follow-up","Ophthalmology consult and observation","Antibiotics alone, then discharge"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The leak is what forces both. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"In which population is ocular trauma the leading cause of monocular blindness, and why does that shape counselling?","choices":["Elderly women, so fall prevention is the message","Infants, so childproofing is the message","Adolescent girls, so sports goggles are the message","Young adult men, so eye protection matters most"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is the group in which it leads. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which type of retinal detachment is NOT treated primarily with surgery?","choices":["Exudative","Rhegmatogenous","All three are surgical","None of the three is surgical"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With no break and no traction, treatment is directed at the underlying condition. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A patient has a periorbital haematoma with no history of injury. Which cause should be sought?","choices":["Anticoagulants, eye surgery or orbital varices","A corneal abrasion earlier in the week","Seasonal allergy","A previous hyphema"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is not always traumatic. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A nail is protruding from a patient's eye. What is done with it?","choices":["Remove it and irrigate the tract","Remove it only if the patient is stable","Remove it under topical anaesthetic","Leave it in place"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It may be tamponading the wound, and removal can extrude intraocular contents. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A foreign body sits in the posterior segment at initial evaluation. What is done?","choices":["Apply a magnet to draw it forward","Leave it alone for now","Remove it under topical anaesthetic","Irrigate it out with balanced salt solution"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Retrieving it early risks more damage to ocular structures. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 16"}]};
